{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it was on the next day.</b> Concerning the arrival of Jethro." "<b>And Moses sat.</b> As was his custom." "<b>From morning until evening.</b> Know that the true morning is when the sun shines; it is also called morning when the pillar of dawn rises, when light begins to appear in the clouds, as I have already mentioned. And this morning is by way of circumstance. And the true evening is at sunset; it is also called evening by way of circumstance until the fading of the light. Thus David said: The sun rises, they gather (Psalms 104:22), and after it is written: Man goes out to his work and to his labor until evening (ibid. 104:23). Therefore: From evening until evening shall you rest your Sabbath (Leviticus 23:32). And it ought to have been from true evening until true evening, except that our Sages, of blessed memory, added from the profane onto the sacred, and said that the departure of the Sabbath day is until the emergence of the stars. Now, it is fitting to bring in the Sabbath, by Torah law, from sunset. And all the scholars of astronomy and all the scholars of the stars agree that the boundary of the day is from the moment the circle of the sun is opposite the surface of the earth in every place, and this is: from its going out until its coming."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:35:36.906239851Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}